text:
Maya (CS): Ravi from Sparrow reported onboarding flakiness: many 429s during first minute after deploy. Logs attached below.
Ravi (Client): yep, our web app opens 4 sockets on start. We mint ephemeral tokens from our auth service for each tab. Token TTL is 20s.
Dev (SE): 20s TTL + multi-tab cold starts is a classic preflight stampede. Can you paste the init pattern?
Ravi (Client): simplified: 
```js
async function start(){
 const t = await fetch('/token').then(r=>r.text());
 await Promise.all([api.call(t), api.call(t), api.call(t), api.call(t)]);
}
```
Maya (CS): short-lived tokens prevent gateway coalescing by key and make per-key throttles kick in.
Polly (SRE): our throttle policy treats keys with TTL <30s as high-risk and keeps a sharp per-key rate cap to protect the cluster. That matches the 429 pattern.
Dev (SE): short-term fixes: 1) avoid Promise.all at init, 2) implement singleflight token fetch per origin, 3) cache tokens locally until expiry minus a small buffer, 4) add jittered backoff on 429. Example singleflight snippet: 
```js
let pending=null;
function fetchToken(){
 if(pending) return pending;
 pending = fetch('/token').then(r=>r.text()).finally(()=> pending=null);
 return pending;
}
```
Ravi (Client): would switching to session tokens (~10m) be supported? We have security concerns about longer-lived tokens.
Polly (SRE): yes we can support session tokens for onboarding or dev builds; recommend 10m for browser clients. For stricter security, use refresh token + bound session but avoid re-issuing for every request.
Maya (CS): as a short-term operational measure, do you want us to temporarily lift your per-key throttle?
Ravi (Client): please, a temporary bump would help while we roll the client change across canary users.
Polly (SRE): granted temporary bump. QuotaBot: updated per-key rate limit for customer=Sparrow, key=web-abc from 100/min to 800/min, expires in 36h.
QuotaBot: change applied by Polly.
Ravi (Client): deployed singleflight + cache to staging; initial verification: 429s down from 15% of requests to 1.2%. Cold-start still shows occasional 429s but much improved.
Dev (SE): follow-up: add client-side clock skew check and log token exp so we can see reuse windows. Also add a small leaky-bucket to smooth bursts.
Maya (CS): I'll schedule a 20min enablement call to go over SDK best practices and token TTL tradeoffs.
Ravi (Client): thanks team, appreciate the quick help :pray:. Will monitor and report back.
Polly (SRE): leaving note in the customer ticket about the temporary bump and recommend revert after verified.
Maya (CS): marking resolved for now; reopen if the 429 percentage exceeds 2% after rollback.
